A||||6||2M4 Dale||Wood Stoves and Pole Barns||||1||
Z||000000||2M4 Dale||09-11-2001||01:34 PM||||Has anyone had any experiance with installing a wood stove into a pole barn......I have a fairly large wood stove with a blower that I plan on putting into my 32'X 40'pole barn. The roof and the side walls are insulated.......will a wood burner keep things warm in there when the temperature goes down to zero or am I gonna have to install a ceiling ??? Also....why is that dog-gone insulated stove pipe so darn expensive ??? Does anyone have some used stuff they would like to sell ??? I need 8" pipe I think !||||reg||1
Z||000001||LarryB||09-11-2001||09:22 PM||||I'm used to more like 24 x 20 and 24 x 32, but yeah, it's doable since you're insulating the roof and walls.  You'll have a better chance of making it work if you mount 1 or 2 ceiling fans on the roof to push down the warmer air.  If you don't do that there's a good chance you'll find it over 90 degrees at the peak of the roof but maybe 40 near the floor. <P>Use good hardwood if you can when it's really cold too, the softer woods like birch, poplar, and beech burn well but don't throw off anywhere as much heat as oak or maple.  Or, if it's legal where you live and the stove is certified to be able to handle it consider using coal when it gets really cold.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-11-2001||11:53 PM||||They make stoves certified for coal and wood.... Use only a stove certified for the fuel(s) you will use. The certification affects both safety and efficiency.<P>ceiling fans will help...<P>You probably only need insulated pipe for the upper section of the stack once you get within X inches of the roof and wood joists. If it's a truss roof you may need insulated pipe from X inches below the bottom cord of the truss. you'll have to check local fire codes. There are allot of different laws about chimneys.<P>You don't have to worry only about the chimeny. Stove location is also regulated. You may be required to keep the whole stove allot further away from walls, etc, than the stove's instructions indicate. you may also be required to install thermal barrier material, esentially an conctrete sheet product, between the stove and walls or floors.<P>There are all kinds of laws governing this. even in rural areas where many people don't expect it.<P>Depending on the size of the barn.... It might be safer and less expencive long term to install one of the new high efficency oil/gas furnaces. Most of those use plastic pipe for "chimney" and fresh air intake, and the heater can go right against walls.<P>At the very least, I'd install two layers of 5/8 inch "fire code" sheet rock between the stove and anything flamable. Even if it's not legally required. (stager and tape the joints of both layers.)<P>The other large concern with a stove is that you have an esentially open flame in a work area that could accumulate flamable gasses.... If you have a fuel spill with a furnace you can simply kill power to it before the fumes build up to explosive levels. Can't do a rapid shut down on a stove. Throwing water in a hot stove can make the stove and/or it's liner material explode.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||09-12-2001||06:07 AM||||I used a wood/coal stove to heat a rather large two story cottage. It was a Vermont Casting Rocky Mountain heater with a catalytic converter to burn the wood gases.  It was actually undersized, but I kept it cranking and with the ceiling fans, that kept the place warm and toasty.  Never did use coal.  A little work refilling the woodbox.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||09-12-2001||09:07 AM||||Heating with wood warms twice. The cutting and the burning.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Provide a floor level exterior source of air for the wood heater, otherwise the air that exits the stove pipe will be replaced with air entering around windows and doors.||||reg||1
Z||000005||2M4 Dale||09-13-2001||10:20 AM||||Thanks for all your response !!! I do have a fairly large cieling fan that I plan to install. My stove is rated for wood or coal. I am planing to have a professional wood stove heating company come and take a look-see for me on Monday........Hey it's only $$$$$$$$ right .... lol. Doesn't really matter my barn is useless to me without winter heat !!!!!||||reg||7
Z||000006||rogergarrison||09-13-2001||10:44 AM||||I have a friend that has a large building he heats with a used oil burning heater (its free) its made out of a couple of 55 gal drums. Its only got 2 reg stovepipes one for incoming air and a vent. It keeps it 75* in the winter.||||reg||1
